# Keep credentials out of the deployment bundle.
#
# Until this file existed, `vercel --prod` uploaded the whole directory —
# .gitignore has no say in what Vercel ships — so .env went up with every
# deploy and dotenv loaded it at runtime. That is how production reached
# Atlas without a single Vercel environment variable being set, and it put
# the Mongo password inside an artifact anyone who can read the project's
# source could open.
#
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# BEFORE THE NEXT DEPLOY: the variables have to exist in Vercel's own
# Environment Variables, or that deploy comes up with nothing to read and
# every request 503s on a Mongo connect that has no URI.
#
#     make push-env          # then redeploy
#
# To undo in a hurry, delete this file and redeploy — the old behaviour
# comes straight back.
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.env
.env.local

# Holds live credentials in plaintext by design. It is a local one-shot
# for seeding Vercel's environment and has no business running there.
push-env.js
